/* Shortcut Press — Version Control with Git & GitHub guide content (generated by tools/site/build.mjs). */
window.SP_GUIDE = {"id":"git-github","slug":"git-github","title":"Version Control with Git & GitHub","software":"Git and GitHub","apps":["Git","VS Code"],"intro":"Learn Git from the first commit to collaborating on GitHub: commits and the staging area, looking back and undoing safely, branches and merges, resolving conflicts, and pushing, pulling and pull requests. You'll use the command line, with notes on doing the same in VS Code.","platformNote":"Commands are the same on Windows (Git Bash or PowerShell), Mac and Linux. Shortcuts are shown for VS Code on Windows and Linux; on a Mac, use Cmd in place of Ctrl.","files":[{"name":"index.html","title":"Practice web page","desc":"A one-page garden website to track with Git.","size":"1 KB"},{"name":"style.css","title":"Practice stylesheet","desc":"The page's styles, used in the branching lesson.","size":"1 KB"},{"name":"README.md","title":"README","desc":"A short project description in Markdown, as GitHub shows on a repository's front page.","size":"1 KB"},{"name":"todo.txt","title":"Private to-do file","desc":"A personal file to keep out of the repository with .gitignore.","size":"1 KB"}],"shortcuts":[{"keys":"Ctrl+Shift+G","action":"Open the Source Control view (VS Code)","app":"VS Code"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Enter","action":"Commit the staged changes (in the Source Control message box)","app":"VS Code"},{"keys":"Ctrl+`","action":"Show or hide the terminal (VS Code)","app":"VS Code"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Shift+P","action":"Command palette (VS Code)","app":"VS Code"},{"keys":"ArrowUp","action":"Bring back the previous command (terminal)","app":"Terminal"},{"keys":"Tab","action":"Complete a file or branch name (terminal)","app":"Terminal"},{"keys":"Q","action":"Leave the git log or diff pager","app":"Terminal"},{"keys":"Space","action":"Next page in the git log pager","app":"Terminal"},{"keys":"Ctrl+C","action":"Cancel the running command (terminal)","app":"Terminal"},{"keys":"Ctrl+L","action":"Clear the terminal screen","app":"Terminal","practice":false,"note":"Your browser uses this shortcut for the address bar."}],"lessons":[{"id":"setup","title":"What Git does and setting it up","summary":"Understand repositories, commits and history, install Git, tell it your name, and turn a folder into a repository.","diagram":{"type":"flow","nodes":["Working folder","Staging area","Commit (snapshot)","History"],"highlight":2,"title":"Git saves snapshots you choose, with a message"},"steps":[{"title":"Snapshots with messages","body":"<p>Git records your project as a series of <strong>commits</strong>: snapshots of every tracked file, each with a message saying what changed and why. You can look at any earlier snapshot, compare two, or go back. The project folder plus its history is a <strong>repository</strong> (repo).</p>"},{"title":"Install and check","body":"<p>Install Git from git-scm.com (on Windows, accept the defaults; you'll get Git Bash too). Open a terminal (Git Bash, PowerShell, Terminal on a Mac, or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>`</kbd> in VS Code) and run <code>git --version</code> to check it's installed.</p>"},{"title":"Tell Git who you are","body":"<p>Every commit records an author. Set yours once per computer:</p><p><code>git config --global user.name \"Your Name\"</code><br><code>git config --global user.email \"you@example.org\"</code></p><p>Use the email address you'll use for GitHub. <code>git config --global init.defaultBranch main</code> names new repositories' first branch “main”.</p>"},{"title":"Create a repository","body":"<p>Make a folder called <em>garden-site</em>, put the four practice files in it, and in a terminal go there with <code>cd</code>. Run <code>git init</code>. Git creates a hidden <code>.git</code> folder that holds the history; the rest of the folder is your normal working files.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a commit?","options":["A file upload","A snapshot of tracked files with a message","A branch","A backup to the cloud"],"answer":1,"explain":"Commits are snapshots in the repository's history."},{"q":"Where does Git store a repository's history?","options":["On GitHub only","In a hidden .git folder inside the project","In each file","In VS Code settings"],"answer":1,"explain":"git init creates the .git folder that holds every commit."}],"exercise":{"title":"Set up your first repository","task":"<p>Install Git, set your name and email, create a <em>garden-site</em> folder with the practice files, and run <code>git init</code> and <code>git status</code>. What does status say about the files?</p>","files":["index.html","style.css","README.md","todo.txt"],"solution":["<code>git --version</code> prints a version number.","<code>git config --global user.name \"Your Name\"</code> and <code>git config --global user.email \"you@example.org\"</code>.","<code>cd</code> into garden-site, then <code>git init</code>: “Initialized empty Git repository…”.","<code>git status</code> lists all four files under <strong>Untracked files</strong>: Git sees them but isn't tracking them yet."]}},{"id":"commits","title":"Staging and committing","summary":"Choose what goes into a commit with the staging area, write useful commit messages, keep private files out with .gitignore, and read the log.","diagram":{"type":"terminal","shell":"bash","lines":[["$","git add index.html style.css README.md"],["$","git commit -m \"Add the first version of the site\""],["","[main (root-commit) 3f2a9c1] Add the first version of the site"],["$","git log --oneline"],["","3f2a9c1 (HEAD -> main) Add the first version of the site"]],"highlight":[1]},"steps":[{"title":"The staging area","body":"<p>Committing is two steps. <code>git add &lt;file&gt;</code> puts a file's current state in the <strong>staging area</strong>: the list of changes for the next commit. <code>git commit</code> saves everything staged as one snapshot. Staging lets you commit related changes together even if you've edited several things.</p>"},{"title":"Commit with a message","body":"<p><code>git commit -m \"Add the first version of the site\"</code>. Good messages say what changed in the imperative mood, as if giving an instruction: “Add opening hours”, “Fix the heading color”. Your future self reading the log will thank you.</p>"},{"title":"Ignore what shouldn't be tracked","body":"<p>Create a file named <code>.gitignore</code> containing the line <code>todo.txt</code>. Git now leaves todo.txt alone: it won't appear in <code>git status</code> and can't be added by accident. Commit the .gitignore itself so the rule is shared. Typical entries: secrets, logs, build output, <code>node_modules/</code>.</p>"},{"title":"Read the history","body":"<p><code>git log</code> lists commits newest first, with author, date and message (press <kbd>Q</kbd> to leave it). <code>git log --oneline</code> shows one line each with a short ID (a hash such as 3f2a9c1; yours will differ). In VS Code, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>G</kbd> opens Source Control, where the same steps are buttons.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does git add do?","options":["Creates a commit","Stages a file's current changes for the next commit","Uploads to GitHub","Creates a new file"],"answer":1,"explain":"add stages; commit saves the staged changes."},{"q":"Which is the best commit message?","options":["stuff","Changes","Add Saturday opening hours to the home page","fixed it!!!"],"answer":2,"explain":"It says exactly what changed, in the imperative mood."}],"exercise":{"title":"Make the first two commits","task":"<p>Add a .gitignore that ignores todo.txt, commit it on its own, then commit the three site files with a clear message. Finally edit index.html to add a line about the harvest festival and commit that. How many commits does <code>git log --oneline</code> show?</p>","files":["index.html","todo.txt"],"solution":["Create .gitignore containing <code>todo.txt</code>; <code>git status</code> no longer lists todo.txt.","<code>git add .gitignore</code> and <code>git commit -m \"Ignore personal notes\"</code>.","<code>git add index.html style.css README.md</code> and <code>git commit -m \"Add the first version of the site\"</code>.","Edit index.html, then <code>git add index.html</code> and <code>git commit -m \"Mention the harvest festival\"</code>.","<code>git log --oneline</code> shows <strong>3 commits</strong>, newest first."]}},{"id":"history","title":"Looking back and undoing","summary":"See exactly what changed with diff and show, throw away unwanted edits, unstage files, and undo a commit safely with revert.","diagram":{"type":"table","head":["You want to…","Command"],"rows":[["See unstaged changes","git diff"],["See staged changes","git diff --staged"],["Discard edits to a file","git restore file"],["Unstage a file","git restore --staged file"],["Undo a past commit","git revert <id>"]],"highlight":{"rows":[4]},"caption":"Safe undo commands"},"steps":[{"title":"What changed?","body":"<p><code>git diff</code> shows edits you haven't staged: removed lines start with <code>-</code>, added lines with <code>+</code>. <code>git diff --staged</code> shows what's staged for the next commit. <code>git show &lt;id&gt;</code> shows what a past commit changed.</p>"},{"title":"Throw away edits","body":"<p>Made a mess of a file and want it back as it was at the last commit? <code>git restore index.html</code>. This really discards your edits; there's no undo, so check with <code>git diff</code> first.</p>"},{"title":"Unstage","body":"<p>Staged something by mistake? <code>git restore --staged style.css</code> takes it out of the staging area but keeps your edits in the file. <code>git status</code> always tells you which command to use for each situation.</p>"},{"title":"Undo a commit with revert","body":"<p><code>git revert &lt;id&gt;</code> makes a <em>new</em> commit that does the opposite of an old one. History is kept and nothing is lost, which makes it safe even after you've shared your commits. (<code>git reset</code> can remove commits from history, but it's best avoided on shared branches until you're confident.)</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"You staged style.css by mistake but want to keep your edits. Which command?","options":["git restore style.css","git restore --staged style.css","git revert style.css","git rm style.css"],"answer":1,"explain":"--staged unstages without touching the file's contents."},{"q":"Why is git revert safe for commits you've already shared?","options":["It deletes nothing and adds a new commit that undoes the old one","It only works locally","It asks for confirmation","It makes a backup"],"answer":0,"explain":"Revert adds history rather than rewriting it."}],"exercise":{"title":"Undo the festival","task":"<p>Change the heading color in style.css, look at the change with <code>git diff</code>, then throw it away with <code>git restore</code>. Next, undo your “Mention the harvest festival” commit with <code>git revert</code>. What does the log look like afterwards?</p>","files":["style.css"],"solution":["Edit the h1 color in style.css; <code>git diff</code> shows the old line with - and the new one with +.","<code>git restore style.css</code>; <code>git diff</code> now shows nothing.","<code>git log --oneline</code> to find the festival commit's short ID, then <code>git revert &lt;id&gt;</code>. Git opens an editor with a message; save and close it (in the default editor Vim, type <code>:wq</code> and press Enter).","The log now shows <strong>4 commits</strong>: the newest is “Revert \"Mention the harvest festival\"”, and the festival line is gone from index.html."]}},{"id":"branches","title":"Branches and merging","summary":"Work on a change in a branch without disturbing main, switch between branches, and merge the work back in.","diagram":{"type":"tree","root":"main","items":[["commit: first version",1],["branch: opening-hours",1,true],["commit: add hours section",2],["commit: style the hours",2],["merge back into main",1]],"caption":"A branch is a separate line of commits"},"steps":[{"title":"Why branches","body":"<p>A branch is an independent line of commits. Do new work on a branch, and <em>main</em> stays working while you experiment. If the idea doesn't pan out, delete the branch; if it does, merge it in.</p>"},{"title":"Create and switch","body":"<p><code>git switch -c opening-hours</code> creates a branch and switches to it. <code>git branch</code> lists branches with a * next to the current one. Commits you make now go on opening-hours only; <code>git switch main</code> takes you back, and the files change to match.</p>"},{"title":"Merge","body":"<p>Switch to the branch you want to merge <em>into</em> (<code>git switch main</code>), then <code>git merge opening-hours</code>. If main hasn't changed since the branch started, Git simply moves main forward: a <em>fast-forward</em>. Otherwise it creates a merge commit joining the two lines.</p>"},{"title":"Tidy up","body":"<p>After merging, <code>git branch -d opening-hours</code> deletes the branch name (its commits stay in main's history). <code>git log --oneline --graph --all</code> draws the branches and merges as a small diagram in the terminal.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"To merge opening-hours into main, which branch must you be on?","options":["opening-hours","main","Either","A new branch"],"answer":1,"explain":"git merge brings the named branch into the current one."},{"q":"What's a fast-forward merge?","options":["A merge that skips conflicts","main simply moves forward because it hasn't changed since the branch started","A merge without commits","A forced push"],"answer":1,"explain":"No merge commit is needed when main has no new commits of its own."}],"exercise":{"title":"Add opening hours on a branch","task":"<p>Create a branch called opening-hours, add an opening-hours paragraph to index.html and commit, then style it in style.css and commit again. Switch back to main and check the paragraph isn't there; then merge the branch and delete it.</p>","files":["index.html","style.css"],"solution":["<code>git switch -c opening-hours</code>","Add <code>&lt;p class=\"hours\"&gt;Open daily 8am to dusk.&lt;/p&gt;</code>; <code>git add index.html</code>; <code>git commit -m \"Add opening hours\"</code>.","Add a <code>.hours { font-weight: bold; }</code> rule; commit it with “Style the opening hours”.","<code>git switch main</code>: the paragraph disappears from the file. <code>git merge opening-hours</code>: Git reports a fast-forward, and it's back.","<code>git branch -d opening-hours</code>."]}},{"id":"conflicts","title":"Resolving merge conflicts","summary":"See why conflicts happen, read the conflict markers, choose the right version, and finish the merge.","diagram":{"type":"code","lang":"index.html during a conflict","lines":["<<<<<<< HEAD","<p>Work parties every Saturday at 10am.</p>","=======","<p>Work parties every Sunday at 9am.</p>",">>>>>>> sunday-parties"],"highlight":[0,2,4],"notes":[[1,"your branch (main)"],[3,"the branch being merged"]]},"steps":[{"title":"Why conflicts happen","body":"<p>Git merges changes to different lines automatically. When two branches change the <em>same</em> lines differently, Git can't know which to keep, so it stops and asks you. Conflicts are normal and fixable, not a sign something broke.</p>"},{"title":"Read the markers","body":"<p>Git marks the conflict in the file. Between <code>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; HEAD</code> and <code>=======</code> is the version on your current branch; between <code>=======</code> and <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; branch-name</code> is the incoming version. <code>git status</code> lists conflicted files as “both modified”.</p>"},{"title":"Resolve","body":"<p>Edit the file to what it should be: keep one version, the other, or a mix, and delete all three marker lines. VS Code highlights conflicts and offers <em>Accept Current</em>, <em>Accept Incoming</em> or <em>Accept Both</em> buttons above each one.</p>"},{"title":"Finish the merge","body":"<p><code>git add index.html</code> marks the conflict as resolved, then <code>git commit</code> completes the merge (Git suggests a message). If you'd rather start again, <code>git merge --abort</code> puts everything back as it was before the merge.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"When does Git report a merge conflict?","options":["Whenever two branches change any file","When both branches change the same lines differently","When a file is deleted","When you merge into main"],"answer":1,"explain":"Changes to different lines merge automatically."},{"q":"After editing a conflicted file, what tells Git it's resolved?","options":["Saving the file","git add on the file","git push","git merge --abort"],"answer":1,"explain":"Staging the file marks the conflict resolved; then commit."}],"exercise":{"title":"Create and fix a conflict","task":"<p>On a branch called sunday-parties, change “Saturday at 10am” to “Sunday at 9am” and commit. On main, change the same line to “Saturday at 9am” and commit. Merge sunday-parties into main, resolve the conflict to “Saturday and Sunday at 9am”, and finish the merge.</p>","files":["index.html"],"solution":["<code>git switch -c sunday-parties</code>, edit the line, <code>git commit -am \"Move work parties to Sunday\"</code> (<code>-a</code> stages tracked files that changed).","<code>git switch main</code>, edit the same line to “Saturday at 9am”, <code>git commit -am \"Start work parties at 9am\"</code>.","<code>git merge sunday-parties</code>: “CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in index.html”.","Edit the line to <code>&lt;p&gt;Work parties every Saturday and Sunday at 9am.&lt;/p&gt;</code> and delete the three marker lines.","<code>git add index.html</code> then <code>git commit</code>, accepting the suggested message. <code>git log --oneline --graph</code> shows the two lines joining."]}},{"id":"github","title":"Working with GitHub","summary":"Put your repository on GitHub, push and pull changes, clone a repository, and propose changes with a pull request.","diagram":{"type":"cycle","nodes":["Branch","Commit","Push","Pull request","Review & merge","Pull"],"highlight":3,"center":"The GitHub flow"},"steps":[{"title":"Create a repository on GitHub","body":"<p>Sign in at github.com, select <em>New</em> (or the + menu › <em>New repository</em>), name it <em>garden-site</em>, and leave it empty: don't add a README, .gitignore or license, because your local repository already has history. GitHub then shows the commands to connect it.</p>"},{"title":"Connect and push","body":"<p><code>git remote add origin https://github.com/your-name/garden-site.git</code> names the GitHub copy “origin”. <code>git push -u origin main</code> uploads main and remembers the link, so later you only type <code>git push</code>. The first push asks you to sign in; Git for Windows and the GitHub CLI open a browser window for that.</p>"},{"title":"Pull and clone","body":"<p>When the GitHub copy has commits you don't have (from a teammate, or an edit made on github.com), <code>git pull</code> fetches and merges them. To get a repository onto a new computer, <code>git clone &lt;url&gt;</code> downloads it with its full history.</p>"},{"title":"Pull requests","body":"<p>On a team, push a branch (<code>git push -u origin new-photo</code>) and open a <strong>pull request</strong> on GitHub: a page that shows the branch's changes, where others can comment, review and, when ready, select <em>Merge pull request</em>. Afterwards, <code>git switch main</code> and <code>git pull</code> bring the merged work back to your computer.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why create the GitHub repository empty, without a README?","options":["READMEs aren't allowed","Your local repository already has history; an extra commit on GitHub would make the first push fail","It's faster","So it stays private"],"answer":1,"explain":"The histories would differ, so the push would be rejected until you pull and merge."},{"q":"What does git push -u origin main do the first time?","options":["Downloads main","Uploads main to origin and remembers the link for later pushes","Creates a branch","Deletes the remote"],"answer":1,"explain":"-u sets the upstream so plain git push works afterwards."}],"exercise":{"title":"Publish and update via a pull request","task":"<p>Create an empty garden-site repository on GitHub, connect your local repository and push main. Then make a branch called add-contact, add a contact line to index.html, push the branch, open a pull request, merge it on GitHub, and pull the result back to main locally.</p>","files":["index.html","README.md"],"solution":["On GitHub: <em>New repository</em>, name garden-site, no README, <em>Create repository</em>.","<code>git remote add origin https://github.com/your-name/garden-site.git</code> and <code>git push -u origin main</code>; refresh the GitHub page to see your files and README.","<code>git switch -c add-contact</code>, edit, <code>git commit -am \"Add contact details\"</code>, <code>git push -u origin add-contact</code>.","GitHub shows a <em>Compare &amp; pull request</em> banner: open the pull request, then <em>Merge pull request</em> › <em>Confirm merge</em>.","Locally: <code>git switch main</code> and <code>git pull</code>; the contact line is now in main."]}}],"extras":{"project":{"id":"bonus-release","title":"Bonus project: tags, releases and a changelog","summary":"Mark versions with tags, write a changelog from your commit history, and publish a release on GitHub.","diagram":{"type":"tree","root":"main","items":[["v1.0.0: first public site",1,true],["commits since…",2],["v1.1.0: opening hours, contact",1],["Release notes on GitHub",2]],"caption":"Tags name important commits"},"steps":[{"title":"Tag a version","body":"<p><code>git tag -a v1.0.0 -m \"First public version\"</code> marks the current commit. Tags don't move when you add commits, so v1.0.0 always means exactly that snapshot. <code>git tag</code> lists tags; <code>git push origin --tags</code> sends them to GitHub.</p>"},{"title":"A changelog from history","body":"<p><code>git log v1.0.0..HEAD --oneline</code> lists every commit since the tag: the raw material for a changelog. Write a CHANGELOG.md with a heading per version and a line per meaningful change, in plain language for readers rather than developers.</p>"},{"title":"Publish a release","body":"<p>On GitHub, open <em>Releases</em> › <em>Draft a new release</em>, choose the tag, paste the changelog section as the notes and select <em>Publish release</em>. Visitors can download that exact version of the project.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does git log v1.0.0..HEAD show?","options":["All commits","Commits made after the v1.0.0 tag","Commits before v1.0.0","Only tagged commits"],"answer":1,"explain":"The range lists commits reachable from HEAD but not from the tag."},{"q":"Do tags move when you commit?","options":["Yes, like branches","No, a tag always points at the same commit","Only annotated ones","Only on GitHub"],"answer":1,"explain":"Branches move forward; tags stay put."}],"exercise":{"title":"Release v1.1.0","task":"<p>Tag your first commit's state as v1.0.0 (tag the commit by ID), write a CHANGELOG.md entry for everything since, tag the current commit v1.1.0, push the tags and publish a GitHub release for v1.1.0.</p>","files":["README.md"],"solution":["<code>git log --oneline</code> to find the first site commit's ID, then <code>git tag -a v1.0.0 &lt;id&gt; -m \"First public version\"</code>.","<code>git log v1.0.0..HEAD --oneline</code> and write the changes under a “## 1.1.0” heading in CHANGELOG.md; commit it.","<code>git tag -a v1.1.0 -m \"Opening hours and contact details\"</code> and <code>git push origin --tags</code>.","On GitHub: <em>Releases › Draft a new release</em>, choose v1.1.0, paste the notes, <em>Publish release</em>."]}},"quizbank":[{"q":"Which command shows the state of your working folder and staging area?","options":["git log","git status","git show","git branch"],"answer":1,"explain":"git status is the command to run whenever you're unsure."},{"q":"What does git commit -am do?","options":["Commits all files including new ones","Stages changes to already-tracked files and commits them","Amends the last commit","Commits to main"],"answer":1,"explain":"-a stages modified tracked files (not new ones); -m gives the message."},{"q":"Which file tells Git which files to ignore?","options":["ignore.txt",".gitignore",".gitconfig","README.md"],"answer":1,"explain":".gitignore lists patterns of files Git should leave alone."},{"q":"How do you leave the git log pager?","options":["Esc","Q","Ctrl+X","Enter"],"answer":1,"explain":"Press q to quit the pager."},{"q":"What does git switch -c new-idea do?","options":["Deletes new-idea","Creates the branch new-idea and switches to it","Merges new-idea","Clones a repository"],"answer":1,"explain":"-c creates the branch before switching."},{"q":"What does git merge --abort do during a conflict?","options":["Keeps your version","Cancels the merge and restores the state before it","Deletes the branch","Pushes the merge"],"answer":1,"explain":"It undoes the in-progress merge."},{"q":"What is origin?","options":["The first commit","The usual name for the remote repository you cloned from or pushed to","The main branch","A GitHub setting"],"answer":1,"explain":"origin is a nickname for the remote's URL."},{"q":"What does git pull do?","options":["Uploads commits","Fetches commits from the remote and merges them into your branch","Creates a pull request","Deletes local changes"],"answer":1,"explain":"pull = fetch + merge."}]},"versionNote":"Software makers update their programs, so menus and button locations can change between versions. This guide is revised when an update changes the steps, and the revised version appears the next time you open it."};
